      "id": 1,
      "text": "Progreso’s name comes from a post office that opened in 1901, originally requested to be called Toluca but named Progreso instead.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofprogreso.com/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1926 the San Benito and Rio Grande Valley Railway built a mission‑style station in Progreso that closed just five years later in 1931.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofprogreso.com/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original town site sits on land granted in 1790 as part of the Llano Grande Spanish land grant awarded to Juan José Hinojosa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofprogreso.com/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Progreso borders the Mexican town of Nuevo Progreso, which was formerly known as Las Flores.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progreso%2C_Texas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Progreso is included in both the McAllen–Edinburg–Mission and Reynosa–McAllen metropolitan areas.",
